<DocsLayout title="Save a non-binding note in Haft" description="Keep one fact, observation, caveat, or rationale without turning it into a human decision or a claim of truth.">
  <h1>Notes</h1>

  <p>
    Use a Haft note for one fact, observation, caveat, or small piece of
    rationale that later work should remember. Ordinary reasoning is not
    captured automatically.
  </p>

  <pre><code>/h-note Remember that the billing sandbox rate limit is a vendor
observation from 2026-08-08, not a contractual production limit.</code></pre>

  <h2>A note is not a decision</h2>

  <p>
    A note can describe a choice or condition. It cannot make the choice,
    authorize work, approve a specification, or prove that a claim is true. If
    you later choose an option, record that choice through <code>h-decide</code>
    instead of treating old note text as approval.
  </p>

  <h2>What a note can carry</h2>

  <p>
    A note requires a title and can include observations, rationale, source or
    evidence references, affected files, search terms, context, and a
    <code>valid_until</code> date. Use a freshness date when the note should not
    support later work indefinitely.
  </p>

  <p>
    Expiry is an attention signal. It does not delete the note or claim that
    the historical observation never happened.
  </p>

  <h2>Advanced: direct interfaces</h2>

  <pre><code>haft interface note.record --json
haft artifact create note.record --input-file input.json --json</code></pre>

  <p>The MCP form is:</p>

  <pre><code>haft_note(title="...", observations=[...])</code></pre>

  <p>
    A note can exist without an exact project-entity link. When that exact link
    matters, provide the identity and project context explicitly; Haft does not
    infer them from a similar name or search rank.
  </p>

